```java
BinarySearchTree<Integer> intTree = new BinarySearchTree<>();
// ...
for (int value : new int[]{6, 2, 8, 7, 9}) intTree.insert(value);
// ...
try {
    Iterator<Integer> it = intTree.iterator();
    it.next();
    intTree.insert(99);  // <-- this inserts 99 into intTree!
```

After the concurrent modification test catches the exception, `intTree` now contains six elements {2, 6, 7, 8, 9, 99} instead of the original five. Then we create `sameElements` with just {8, 9, 2, 7, 6} and compare the two trees, but they have different sizes now.

The issue is that the size check fails before we even get to the element comparison. I need to either run the equals tests before modifying the tree, or use a separate tree for the concurrent modification test. The cleanest approach is to restructure the test so the concurrent modification check uses its own isolated tree instance. it {2, 6, 7, 8, 9, 99}. Then when I test equality later with a tree containing just the original five elements, it fails because the counts don't match. The cleanest solution is to use a separate tree for the concurrent modification test so it doesn't pollute the state of `intTree`.

